# What a USER download must not contain.
#
# `export-ignore` drops a path from every archive git generates from this
# branch. That single fact covers BOTH ways eSim is handed to a user, so the
# two can never drift apart:
#
#   1. curl one-liner  -> Ubuntu/bootstrap.sh fetches
#      codeload.github.com/.../tar.gz/refs/heads/<branch>, which GitHub builds
#      with `git archive`. It honours these lines server-side.
#   2. release zip     -> make-release.sh archives the same commit locally.
#
# A `git clone` is NOT an archive, so developers still get everything below.
# Nothing here is deleted from the branch -- it just stops shipping.
#
# Anything the installer touches at runtime MUST NOT be listed here. Verified
# load-bearing, keep out of this file: VERSION, src/, library/, images/,
# Examples/, nghdl/, ihp/ (install-eSim.sh runs ihp/ihp-install-script.sh),
# Ubuntu/ (bootstrap.sh's is_esim_tree() requires Ubuntu/install-eSim.sh),
# README.md, LICENSE, INSTALL.

# ---- packaging notes (maintainer-facing) ----
/PACKAGING.md                    export-ignore
/MAINTAINERS-PACKAGING.md        export-ignore

# ---- contributor / repo furniture ----
/CONTRIBUTION.md                 export-ignore
/SECURITY.md                     export-ignore
/.github/                        export-ignore
/.travis.yml                     export-ignore
/.readthedocs.yaml               export-ignore
/.gitignore                      export-ignore
/.gitattributes                  export-ignore
/.DS_Store                       export-ignore

# ---- build & release tooling (the user runs install-eSim.sh, not these) ----
/make-release.sh                 export-ignore
/setup.py                        export-ignore
/requirements.txt                export-ignore
/scripts/                        export-ignore

# patches/ is maintainer-facing EXCEPT patches/ngspice/, which is part of the
# Ubuntu install path: install-nghdl.sh applies it to the extracted
# nghdl-simulator tree. Dropping it from the archive would leave the curl and
# zip installs building an unpatched simulator -- silently, because a missing
# patch directory is not an error. So ignore the maintainer patches by name
# and let patches/ngspice/ ship. (A blanket `/patches/ export-ignore` here
# would take patches/ngspice/ with it.)
/patches/*.patch                 export-ignore

# ---- Sphinx doc sources (the built manual is shipped, not its input) ----
/conf.py                         export-ignore
/index.rst                       export-ignore
/code/                           export-ignore
/docs/                           export-ignore

# ---- Windows-only packaging tree; the curl/zip paths are Ubuntu-only ----
/windows/                        export-ignore

# ---- legacy packaging contributions (preserved, not released for 2.6) ----
/appimage/                       export-ignore
/snap/                           export-ignore

# Preserved legacy text formats contain significant historical whitespace.
appimage/build-appimage.sh whitespace=-trailing-space
snap/snapcraft.yaml whitespace=-trailing-space,-blank-at-eof
src/converter/schematic_converters/ConvertedLibs/*.lib whitespace=-trailing-space
library/kicadLibrary/eSim-symbols/legacy/*.lib whitespace=-trailing-space

# ---- test suites ----
/src/conftest.py                 export-ignore
src/**/tests/                    export-ignore
# The zip drops Ubuntu/ entirely (make-release.sh flattens the installer to
# the root), but the curl path tarballs the branch WITH Ubuntu/ -- so this
# line is what keeps the installer's own tests out of a user download.
/Ubuntu/tests/                   export-ignore

# ---- regeneratable ngspice output committed into the library ----
# 427 MB of the 644 MB tree. eSim rewrites these the first time a circuit is
# simulated; shipping them just makes the download four times bigger.
*.raw                            export-ignore
plot_data_*.txt                  export-ignore

# ---- dead duplicate of library/SubcircuitLibrary (55 MB) ----
# Case-only twin. Every reference in src/ is to the capital-S tree; this one is
# unreachable. (Upstream carries a remove-duplicate-subcircuitlibrary branch.)
/library/subcircuitLibrary/       export-ignore

# MSYS2 sh runs these; CRLF from autocrlf checkout breaks them on fresh clones.
windows/*.sh text eol=lf

# Maintainers also run the release builder from Linux or WSL. Keep its shebang
# and shell syntax usable even when the checkout was created by Windows Git.
/make-release.sh text eol=lf
/VERSION text eol=lf

# Same reasoning, higher stakes: these two ARE the Ubuntu install path. A
# contributor on Windows with core.autocrlf=true checks them out as CRLF, and
# an editor that writes the file back verbatim commits them that way -- which
# ships an installer that dies on the first line with
#   /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
# Pin them to LF in the working tree so that can never happen.
Ubuntu/*.sh text eol=lf

# Same again, for the same reason: install-nghdl.sh feeds these to `patch`
# against an LF source tree. A CRLF checkout on a contributor's Windows box,
# committed back verbatim, produces a patch that no longer applies.
patches/ngspice/*.patch text eol=lf
